<title>RUSSIA: Eight CIS members have industry growth in Jan-Aug.</title>
<headline>Eight CIS members have industry growth in Jan-Aug.</headline>
<dateline>MOSCOW 1996-10-18</dateline>
<text>
<p>Eight members of the Commonwealth of Independent States showed growth in industrial production in the first eight months of 1996, the CIS Interstate Statistics Committee said.</p>
<p>But average ouput among the group of 12 former Soviet republics fell 3.1 percent compared with the same period of 1995. In the first eight months of 1995, industrial output was 6.8 percent lower than in the corresponding 1994 period, the committee said in its latest bulletin.</p>
<p>Gross Domestic Product fell on average by 5.9 percent in the first eight months, after a 5.8 percent fall in the same period of 1995.</p>
<p>The biggest rise in industrial production was recorded in the Central Asian republics of Kyrgyzstan and Turkmenistan.</p>
<p>The CIS comprises all former Soviet republics except the three Baltic states.</p>
<p>The committee issued the following data (percentage change on year earlier, except consumer price inflation which is percentage change on previous month):</p>
<p>		GDP	Industrial production   Inflation</p>
<p>	   Jan-Aug 96  Jan-Aug 96 Jan-Aug 95   Aug 96</p>
<p> Azerbaijan    -2.1	-9.2	 -26.4    +2.0</p>
<p> Armenia	 +4.2	+1.2	  +4.2    +0.3</p>
<p> Belarus	 +0.4	+2.8	 -11.8    +1.3</p>
<p> Georgia*     +10.4	+3.4	 -21.0     0.0</p>
<p> Kazakhstan    +0.1	+0.1	  -7.9    +0.7</p>
<p> Kyrgyzstan    +3.1     +11.9	 -13.9    +0.5</p>
<p> Moldova*	-3.5	+4.5	 -15.3    -0.3</p>
<p> Russia	  -5.8	-5.0	  -3.0    -0.2</p>
<p> Tajikistan   -19.7     -24.8	 -15.0    +1.8</p>
<p> Turkmenistan   n/a     +27.5	 -20.4     n/a</p>
<p> Uzbekistan*   +1.4	+5.6	  -3.9     n/a</p>
<p> Ukraine	 -9.5	-3.7	  -14.1   +5.7</p>
<p>NOTE - Georgia and Uzbekistan GDP are first half, Moldova is first quarter.</p>
<p>Comparisons only available for industrial production.</p>
<p>--Nigel Stephenson, Moscow Newsroom, +7095 941 8520</p>
